00:02:19.690 --> 00:02:22.410
There's a really cool article
about introducing an easy way

00:02:22.410 --> 00:02:25.200
to deploy containers on
Google Compute Engine.

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:27.812
I like this one because,
sometimes, you're like,

00:02:27.812 --> 00:02:28.770
oh, I have a container.

00:02:28.770 --> 00:02:31.020
Maybe you found something
on Docker Hub that you like.

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:33.660
Maybe you want to run Redis
or some sort of Wordpress

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:35.280
instance, or who knows what.

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:37.710
But it's-- on Docker Hub is
an image, but you're like,

00:02:37.710 --> 00:02:39.630
Kubernetes-- that seems
like a lot of work.

00:02:39.630 --> 00:02:42.005
I just want to run this on a
single VM sitting somewhere,

00:02:42.005 --> 00:02:43.046
or maybe even in a group.

00:02:43.046 --> 00:02:44.640
But I just want
something simple.

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:47.580
Now, you can basically go
over to GCE Compute Engine

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:49.559
and say, hey, I'd like
to create an instance,

00:02:49.559 --> 00:02:51.600
and here's the container
I want you to run on it.

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:52.250
Boom, done.

00:02:52.250 --> 00:02:53.250
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:54.190
MARK MANDEL: That's really cool.

00:02:54.190 --> 00:02:55.620
And you can tie that into
managed instance groups,

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:57.090
too, and auto scaling
if you need to,

00:02:57.090 --> 00:02:58.770
but I really think
it's kind of nice just

00:02:58.770 --> 00:03:00.895
to have this really simple
thing where you're like,

00:03:00.895 --> 00:03:03.395
oh, I just need to run an
instance of Redis or something

00:03:03.395 --> 00:03:05.520
like that, and then it's
just very simple to set up

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:07.080
with something out
of hub.docker.com.

