you moved the crossword to App

00:04:39.450 --> 00:04:40.716
Engine and Go.

00:04:40.716 --> 00:04:42.090
That was a really
good blog post,

00:04:42.090 --> 00:04:44.730
not only because it was
about Go, but also because it

00:04:44.730 --> 00:04:45.400
was about games.

00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:46.400
JP ROBINSON: Yeah, yeah.

00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:47.275
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:04:47.275 --> 00:04:49.615
All right, so-- OK, so
let's talk high-level first.

00:04:49.615 --> 00:04:51.990
I'm guessing probably Deep is
the best question for this.

00:04:51.990 --> 00:04:56.150
So New York Times, isn't
this a paper publication?

00:04:56.150 --> 00:04:59.180
Like, what do you actually
do on the internet?

00:04:59.180 --> 00:05:01.530
DEEP KAPADIA: Oh, that's
an interesting one.

00:05:01.530 --> 00:05:06.750
So yes, we started out as a
paper publication in 1851.

00:05:06.750 --> 00:05:08.910
But since then, we've
embraced other ways

00:05:08.910 --> 00:05:12.450
of telling stories, one of them
being telling stories online.

00:05:12.450 --> 00:05:15.630
We, in fact, launched our first
website, believe it or not,

00:05:15.630 --> 00:05:18.862
in 1996, before most
people had a website.

00:05:18.862 --> 00:05:20.100
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:22.810
We've We've had an online
presence for a long time.

00:05:22.810 --> 00:05:24.930
And along the way, we've
sort of transformed

00:05:24.930 --> 00:05:26.820
the way we tell stories.

00:05:26.820 --> 00:05:29.580
Like, we're doing a
lot of publications

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:32.440
just for consumption online.

00:05:32.440 --> 00:05:35.550
So we have a huge
video production team.

00:05:35.550 --> 00:05:41.400
We also have a daily
podcast called The Daily.

00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:44.530
We also have a
virtual reality app.

00:05:44.530 --> 00:05:47.160
If you have an
Android or iOS phone,

00:05:47.160 --> 00:05:49.470
you can download it
from the app stores.

00:05:49.470 --> 00:05:52.350
So there's a lot of things
we've sort of experimented

00:05:52.350 --> 00:05:53.400
with along the way.

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:56.100
But most importantly, we
have our website, which

00:05:56.100 --> 00:05:58.140
is fairly well trafficked.

00:05:58.140 --> 00:05:59.704
We get about--

00:05:59.704 --> 00:06:01.620
I think, our homepage
used to get about, like,

00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:03.060
50 million hits a day.

00:06:03.060 --> 00:06:06.090
I don't know what the latest
number is at this point.

00:06:06.090 --> 00:06:09.191
And then, JP has all his games
apps that he can tell you guys

00:06:09.191 --> 00:06:09.690
about.

00:06:09.690 --> 00:06:10.660
JP ROBINSON: Sure.

00:06:10.660 --> 00:06:13.620
So yeah, beyond, you
know, just servicing up

00:06:13.620 --> 00:06:16.290
the news and all of our news
products associated with

00:06:16.290 --> 00:06:19.560
that, we have had historically,
along with the website,

00:06:19.560 --> 00:06:21.720
our crossword as a
digital offering.

00:06:21.720 --> 00:06:24.449
And a big push from that,
when it comes to technology,

00:06:24.449 --> 00:06:26.490
is that not only are we
serving up the crossword,

00:06:26.490 --> 00:06:29.400
but we allow you to play
the crossword on one device,

00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:32.040
and then we try to seamlessly
sync it to all of your devices

00:06:32.040 --> 00:06:33.050
that may be logged in.

00:06:33.050 --> 00:06:33.540
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:06:33.540 --> 00:06:34.450
JP ROBINSON: So yeah,
it's a lot of stuff.

00:06:34.450 --> 00:06:35.824
And on top of
that, you know, all

00:06:35.824 --> 00:06:37.590
of these products
and services also

00:06:37.590 --> 00:06:39.914
have a layer of
personalization built in.

00:06:39.914 --> 00:06:41.580
So there's obviously
a lot of technology

00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:42.930
backing all of that fun stuff.

00:06:42.930 --> 00:06:45.060
DEEP KAPADIA: And
we're constantly

00:06:45.060 --> 00:06:48.750
trying to come up with new
ways of, like, repurposing

00:06:48.750 --> 00:06:49.780
our content, right?

00:06:49.780 --> 00:06:54.235
So we've always had recipes
in our management system.

00:06:54.235 --> 00:06:55.860
But at one point, we
decided, like, you

00:06:55.860 --> 00:06:57.480
know, how about we build
a cooking app where people

00:06:57.480 --> 00:06:59.400
can have annotations
on their recipes,

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:03.600
and actually share their
recipes with each other, etc.

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:07.200
So somewhere along the line,
we created a new products

00:07:07.200 --> 00:07:10.636
division, which was building
all these experiments and sort

00:07:10.636 --> 00:07:12.510
of trying to see if we
could prioritize them.

00:07:12.510 --> 00:07:14.790
As it stands now,
our cooking app

00:07:14.790 --> 00:07:18.099
has its own stream of revenue.

00:07:18.099 --> 00:07:19.890
It's available on the
app store, and, like,

00:07:19.890 --> 00:07:22.360
you know, people-- you can
just buy a subscription to it.

00:07:22.360 --> 00:07:24.815
So there's definitely
lots of different things.

00:07:24.815 --> 00:07:25.731
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:07:25.731 --> 00:07:28.290
So while you were describing
all of the cool things you do,

00:07:28.290 --> 00:07:31.980
I actually went through
the Internet Archive,

00:07:31.980 --> 00:07:38.010
and I found a screenshot of your
web page on November 12, 1996.

00:07:38.010 --> 00:07:39.340
And I've got to say--

00:07:39.340 --> 00:07:42.780
yeah, I've got to say, I'm
very happy that we've evolved,

00:07:42.780 --> 00:07:45.350
because that's a very,
very cool web page.

00:07:45.350 --> 00:07:46.810
I'll put a link
in the show notes.

00:07:46.810 --> 00:07:50.530
It's actually worth seeing
it back then, and now again.

00:07:50.530 --> 00:07:51.905
MARK MANDEL:
Genuinely fantastic.

00:07:51.905 --> 00:07:53.114
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It is, yeah.

00:07:53.114 --> 00:07:54.946
JP ROBINSON: It truly
was just a single GIF.

00:07:54.946 --> 00:07:55.830
That's all we put up.

00:07:55.830 --> 00:07:57.330
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Yeah, it was a GIF.

00:07:57.330 --> 00:07:58.170
That's it.

00:07:58.170 --> 00:07:59.190
It is pretty cool.

00:07:59.190 --> 00:08:01.034
Anyway, since-- you've
talked a little bit

00:08:01.034 --> 00:08:03.450
about all of the cool things
you do on the New York Times.

00:08:03.450 --> 00:08:05.610
But our audience is,
you know, like-- we

00:08:05.610 --> 00:08:08.100
are into technical details.

00:08:08.100 --> 00:08:10.290
Tell us a little bit about
how you do this, like,

00:08:10.290 --> 00:08:12.540
we know you're using a pension
for some of the things.

00:08:12.540 --> 00:08:14.070
Do you use a pension
for everything?

00:08:14.070 --> 00:08:14.880
How does this work?

00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:16.380
DEEP KAPADIA: No,
actually, we don't

00:08:16.380 --> 00:08:17.970
use App Engine for everything.

00:08:17.970 --> 00:08:20.324
So I mean, we're in a bit
of a transition right now.

00:08:20.324 --> 00:08:21.990
So, you know, as I
said, like, you know,

00:08:21.990 --> 00:08:25.080
we've had a website
since a while now.

00:08:25.080 --> 00:08:27.180
So at some point,
like, our operations

00:08:27.180 --> 00:08:30.090
became large enough for us
to sort of consider, like,

00:08:30.090 --> 00:08:31.810
having our own data centers.

00:08:31.810 --> 00:08:36.091
So we were running in our
co-located data centers--

00:08:36.091 --> 00:08:38.549
like, we have one on the east
coast, one on the west coast,

00:08:38.549 --> 00:08:40.350
and a few peripheral
ones here and there.

00:08:40.350 --> 00:08:42.960
We have one in our
building in Manhattan,

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:47.280
which is quite expensive real
estate for a data center.

00:08:47.280 --> 00:08:51.720
So we've largely been running
applications in our own data

00:08:51.720 --> 00:08:56.250
center, like, you know, with
a layer of virtualization

00:08:56.250 --> 00:08:59.880
on top of the bare metal
that we were running.

00:08:59.880 --> 00:09:03.960
About six years ago, I think,
we decided to start looking at,

00:09:03.960 --> 00:09:08.310
like, this Cloud thing that
people were talking about.

00:09:08.310 --> 00:09:12.520
So we started, like, building
some apps in the Amazon Cloud.

00:09:12.520 --> 00:09:15.070
But you know, one of the
things we tried to do

00:09:15.070 --> 00:09:18.710
was pretty much replicate
what we were doing in our data

00:09:18.710 --> 00:09:21.690
centers in the Cloud, which
was probably not the most

00:09:21.690 --> 00:09:23.050
ideal way to go about it.

00:09:23.050 --> 00:09:25.230
So what we were trying
to do-- at some point,

00:09:25.230 --> 00:09:28.320
we sort of decided we
needed better abstractions

00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:29.430
to build our applications.

00:09:29.430 --> 00:09:32.130
Granted, like, you know, we
were largely a news and media

00:09:32.130 --> 00:09:32.820
organization.

00:09:32.820 --> 00:09:36.870
So I mean, technology
isn't quite our forte.

00:09:36.870 --> 00:09:38.400
Even though we're
pretty good at it,

00:09:38.400 --> 00:09:39.941
we feel that, like,
you know, we want

00:09:39.941 --> 00:09:42.580
to be building more applications
and less infrastructure.

00:09:42.580 --> 00:09:45.950
So that prompted us to
go and, like, you know,

00:09:45.950 --> 00:09:47.580
evaluate what we're doing.

00:09:47.580 --> 00:09:49.350
And at some point, we
decided that we want

00:09:49.350 --> 00:09:51.910
to move entirely to the Cloud.

00:09:51.910 --> 00:09:53.760
So that's where Google comes in.

00:09:53.760 --> 00:09:56.640
And you know, for some
of the applications,

00:09:56.640 --> 00:09:59.370
it seemed like App Engine
was a really good fit,

00:09:59.370 --> 00:10:02.580
especially, you know, JP started
looking at the App Engine

00:10:02.580 --> 00:10:03.870
sometime last year.

00:10:03.870 --> 00:10:07.180
And he found it a very good fit
for some of the applications he

00:10:07.180 --> 00:10:08.320
was building.

00:10:08.320 --> 00:10:10.060
But on the whole,
like, when we look

00:10:10.060 --> 00:10:13.150
at some of the applications we
have, you know, a lot of those

00:10:13.150 --> 00:10:15.050
could be containerized
fairly easily.

00:10:15.050 --> 00:10:19.150
So Google also provided us
with a very good abstraction

00:10:19.150 --> 00:10:21.310
to deploy containers,
which was Google Kontainer

00:10:21.310 --> 00:10:22.640
Engine, or GKE.

00:10:22.640 --> 00:10:24.850
And, you know, it gives
us all the benefits

00:10:24.850 --> 00:10:28.060
of running in Kubernetes
without actually having

00:10:28.060 --> 00:10:29.770
to run Kubernetes ourselves.

00:10:29.770 --> 00:10:31.972
So both the App Engine
and GKE together

00:10:31.972 --> 00:10:33.430
were a bit of a
game changer for us

00:10:33.430 --> 00:10:35.147
on the application
development side.

00:10:35.147 --> 00:10:37.480
And I said, like, you know,
we were running our own data

00:10:37.480 --> 00:10:38.080
centers.

00:10:38.080 --> 00:10:39.310
We also had this
large loop cluster

00:10:39.310 --> 00:10:41.380
that we were doing all
sorts of data processing

00:10:41.380 --> 00:10:43.720
and like, you know,
data analysis.

00:10:43.720 --> 00:10:45.370
At some point,
like, our data team

00:10:45.370 --> 00:10:47.380
decided to try out BigQuery.

00:10:47.380 --> 00:10:49.300
Like, they were
completely blown away

00:10:49.300 --> 00:10:51.930
with all the features it had
and, like, you know how--

00:10:51.930 --> 00:10:53.170
with the ease of use.

00:10:53.170 --> 00:10:56.110
So BigQuery was sort of
like the gateway drug for us

00:10:56.110 --> 00:10:58.690
to start looking at other
Google abstractions.

00:10:58.690 --> 00:11:02.865
And then that led to us using
App Engine and Kubernetes.

00:11:02.865 --> 00:11:05.320
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
We've heard that many times.

00:11:05.320 --> 00:11:08.560
People try BigQuery, and after
that, they're like, oh, oh wow.

00:11:08.560 --> 00:11:09.347
Yeah, that's cool.

00:11:09.347 --> 00:11:10.430
MARK MANDEL: That is cool.

00:11:10.430 --> 00:11:12.850
So I'm actually kind of
curious to kind of probably

00:11:12.850 --> 00:11:14.470
dig a little deeper.

00:11:14.470 --> 00:11:16.736
App Engine, Kubernetes,
this is a common thing

00:11:16.736 --> 00:11:18.110
that I hear from
a lot of people.

00:11:18.110 --> 00:11:21.760
How do you end up choosing which
piece goes on which platform?

00:11:21.760 --> 00:11:24.850
DEEP KAPADIA: At
this point, we don't.

00:11:24.850 --> 00:11:27.150
So in the sense
that, you know, like,

00:11:27.150 --> 00:11:30.580
we sort of give
people the option

00:11:30.580 --> 00:11:33.430
and say, like, figure out
what works for you the best.

00:11:33.430 --> 00:11:37.720
Like, what my team did
was we provided our users

00:11:37.720 --> 00:11:41.200
with a consistent way to
deploy on both engine, as well

00:11:41.200 --> 00:11:42.640
as Kubernetes.

00:11:42.640 --> 00:11:45.100
We use a CI tool called Drone.

00:11:45.100 --> 00:11:47.530
And we host Drone internally.

00:11:47.530 --> 00:11:50.200
And we built a
couple of plug-ins

00:11:50.200 --> 00:11:53.200
that allow us to deploy the
code on GKE or App Engine.

00:11:53.200 --> 00:11:55.932
And both those plug-ins seem
to work, for the most part.

00:11:55.932 --> 00:11:58.140
Like, of course, like, you
know, there's definitely--

00:11:58.140 --> 00:11:59.860
people are always asking
for more and more features,

00:11:59.860 --> 00:12:01.000
and that kind of
stuff, and like,

00:12:01.000 --> 00:12:02.791
for the pipeline to be
more and more fancy.

00:12:02.791 --> 00:12:06.211
People want to do all sorts of
blue-green deploys and things

00:12:06.211 --> 00:12:06.710
like that.

00:12:06.710 --> 00:12:08.756
But for the most
part, it seems to work

00:12:08.756 --> 00:12:10.630
if you want to get your
code from your laptop

00:12:10.630 --> 00:12:11.830
to App Engine or GKE.

00:12:11.830 --> 00:12:14.980
And then they decide
what works best for them.

00:12:14.980 --> 00:12:17.230
Typically, if it's
a go application,

00:12:17.230 --> 00:12:20.980
I often see JP
sort of swooping in

00:12:20.980 --> 00:12:24.475
and, like, giving them the spiel
about all the benefits of going

00:12:24.475 --> 00:12:26.350
on App Engine Standard,
et cetera, et cetera.

00:12:26.350 --> 00:12:28.210
And he can tell you
more about that.

00:12:28.210 --> 00:12:31.390
But that's typically how things
have been working so far.

00:12:31.390 --> 00:12:33.460
MARK MANDEL: JP, did
you want to weigh in?

00:12:33.460 --> 00:12:34.210
JP ROBINSON: Sure.

00:12:34.210 --> 00:12:36.420
So yeah, I mean, it really
comes down to, you know,

00:12:36.420 --> 00:12:39.610
what's the basic thing
you're Building, oftentimes.

00:12:39.610 --> 00:12:41.920
For us, it's just a simple
API or something like that,

00:12:41.920 --> 00:12:44.740
I need to store some data and
retrieve it later-- that kind

00:12:44.740 --> 00:12:45.560
of stuff.

00:12:45.560 --> 00:12:48.580
And a lot of times, it really
just comes down to App Engine

00:12:48.580 --> 00:12:50.290
and Data Store is really enough.

00:12:50.290 --> 00:12:52.030
I find that we really--

00:12:52.030 --> 00:12:53.569
we still make GKE
an option, in case

00:12:53.569 --> 00:12:55.360
you need to do something
crazy or something

00:12:55.360 --> 00:12:57.910
that's not necessarily
API-service-oriented.

00:12:57.910 --> 00:13:00.100
Like, even for my
work, sometimes, like,

00:13:00.100 --> 00:13:02.500
I have a process that just
needs to generate PDFs.

00:13:02.500 --> 00:13:04.210
I can't really do
that in App Engine.

00:13:04.210 --> 00:13:06.670
So I just have something
listening to a Pub/Sub,

00:13:06.670 --> 00:13:09.550
generating PDFs, and spitting
them back on Pub/Sub.

00:13:09.550 --> 00:13:10.950
So it's really, like--

00:13:10.950 --> 00:13:13.060
if it's something basic,
something simple that you

00:13:13.060 --> 00:13:15.137
can build in, like, a
day, a lot of times,

00:13:15.137 --> 00:13:16.720
I will just point
people to App Engine

00:13:16.720 --> 00:13:18.511
and they don't have to
worry about anything

00:13:18.511 --> 00:13:19.374
beyond the product.

00:13:19.374 --> 00:13:21.040
And Kubernetes if you
want to get crazy.

00:13:21.040 --> 00:13:21.956
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:13:21.956 --> 00:13:24.040
I'm curious about the
developer experience.

00:13:24.040 --> 00:13:27.467
You told us a little bit
about the Drone plug-ins

00:13:27.467 --> 00:13:29.050
that you used at--
by the way, we also

00:13:29.050 --> 00:13:31.220
used as a thing of the
week, a long time ago.

00:13:31.220 --> 00:13:32.260
MARK MANDEL: They do
seem to show up a lot.

00:13:32.260 --> 00:13:33.520
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
because they're open-source,

00:13:33.520 --> 00:13:34.900
and we love open-source.

00:13:34.900 --> 00:13:36.670
Could you tell us a
bit more about how

00:13:36.670 --> 00:13:38.860
does it look like when
you're actually testing?

00:13:38.860 --> 00:13:43.240
Do you have a QA environment
different to the App Engine--

00:13:43.240 --> 00:13:45.340
like, do you have different
App Engine projects?

00:13:45.340 --> 00:13:47.680
How do you manage all of this?

00:13:47.680 --> 00:13:49.720
JP ROBINSON: So from an
App Engine perspective,

00:13:49.720 --> 00:13:52.510
we generally have a
project per environment.

00:13:52.510 --> 00:13:54.010
I've got one for,
like, our staging,

00:13:54.010 --> 00:13:56.440
and one for production, and one
for, like, our sandbox or lab,

00:13:56.440 --> 00:13:57.864
or whatever you want to call it.

00:13:57.864 --> 00:13:59.530
And we do, like, just
a normal get flow.

00:13:59.530 --> 00:14:01.660
You know, you'll check
in a feature branch.

00:14:01.660 --> 00:14:03.850
Once that gets blessed onto
our development branch,

00:14:03.850 --> 00:14:05.800
it will get pushed out
to one environment.

00:14:05.800 --> 00:14:07.341
Once it's merged
into Master, it will

00:14:07.341 --> 00:14:08.720
go to the staging environment.

00:14:08.720 --> 00:14:10.900
And then, we will tag
the repo with a release.

00:14:10.900 --> 00:14:13.180
And that will kick off
a production deployment.

00:14:13.180 --> 00:14:14.471
So it's pretty straightforward.

00:14:14.471 --> 00:14:16.870
And it's very similar
for GKE or App Engine,

00:14:16.870 --> 00:14:19.600
really just kind of get oriented
and kind of event driven.

00:14:19.600 --> 00:14:21.660
DEEP KAPADIA: What JP said.

00:14:21.660 --> 00:14:23.350
MARK MANDEL: As we
mentioned before,

00:14:23.350 --> 00:14:25.660
you open-source the
plug-ins for Drone.

00:14:25.660 --> 00:14:28.064
So I'm curious, as an
organization, is, like,

00:14:28.064 --> 00:14:29.980
open-source something
that's important to you?

00:14:29.980 --> 00:14:31.566
Or has it just been,
like, you thought

00:14:31.566 --> 00:14:33.190
these might be useful
for other people?

00:14:33.190 --> 00:14:34.920
What was the story with that?

00:14:34.920 --> 00:14:36.670
DEEP KAPADIA: Both.

00:14:36.670 --> 00:14:41.914
We definitely want to be a part
of the open-source community.

00:14:41.914 --> 00:14:43.330
But at the same
time, we also want

00:14:43.330 --> 00:14:47.830
to be using tools that
can be supported widely.

00:14:47.830 --> 00:14:50.110
So that was one of the
very big reasons for us

00:14:50.110 --> 00:14:53.090
to sort of look at Drone
in the first place.

00:14:53.090 --> 00:14:55.930
And then on top of
that, like, I think,

00:14:55.930 --> 00:14:58.740
once we found out that this
could be completely useful--

00:14:58.740 --> 00:15:02.030
and there's nothing proprietary
about a fairly simple plug-in

00:15:02.030 --> 00:15:05.840
that, like, you know, just
deploys code to a set of GKE

00:15:05.840 --> 00:15:06.980
instances--

00:15:06.980 --> 00:15:11.020
it was pretty much a non-brainer
for us to say, like, you know,

00:15:11.020 --> 00:15:12.780
let's just open-source it.

00:15:12.780 --> 00:15:15.810
But on the whole, we do find
ourselves embracing a lot

00:15:15.810 --> 00:15:17.930
of open standards and
open-source technologies .

00:15:17.930 --> 00:15:23.370
Like, one of the big reasons
for us to sort of buy into GKE

00:15:23.370 --> 00:15:26.850
was Kubernetes itself
being an open standard.

00:15:26.850 --> 00:15:28.290
As I said, like,
you know, we did

00:15:28.290 --> 00:15:29.760
dabble in AWS for a long time.

00:15:29.760 --> 00:15:33.520
And a lot of our workloads
still run in AWS.

00:15:33.520 --> 00:15:35.550
I know this is a GCP podcast.

00:15:35.550 --> 00:15:38.230
But the thing is, like,
something like Kubernetes

00:15:38.230 --> 00:15:41.490
would maybe, at some point,
allow us to run workloads

00:15:41.490 --> 00:15:42.960
in AWS, as well as GCP.

00:15:42.960 --> 00:15:44.800
So it's a fairly open standard.

00:15:44.800 --> 00:15:45.396
It's portable.

00:15:45.396 --> 00:15:47.520
As long as we're deploying
to containers, you know,

00:15:47.520 --> 00:15:51.070
we should be able to move our
workloads just about anywhere.

00:15:51.070 --> 00:15:52.320
So that was one of the things.

00:15:52.320 --> 00:15:57.030
And the other thing is we use
a lot of tools from HashiCorp.

00:15:57.030 --> 00:15:58.320
They're all written in Go.

00:15:58.320 --> 00:16:01.770
So you know, at least on
the deployment automation

00:16:01.770 --> 00:16:04.110
side of things, you're
trying to use a lot of Go.

00:16:04.110 --> 00:16:07.760
And a lot of the Go community is
already embracing open-source.

00:16:07.760 --> 00:16:08.470
It's online.

00:16:08.470 --> 00:16:10.410
And you know, we're often
contributing to some

00:16:10.410 --> 00:16:12.390
of the HashiCorp products.

00:16:12.390 --> 00:16:14.550
So we do contribute
to Terraform.

00:16:14.550 --> 00:16:16.890
We've contributed to
a couple other things.

00:16:16.890 --> 00:16:19.290
We've also contributed to
Kubernetes a little bit.

00:16:19.290 --> 00:16:20.940
So it allows us to
also sort of shape

00:16:20.940 --> 00:16:22.950
the direction of some of
these things, as well.

00:16:22.950 --> 00:16:24.658
JP ROBINSON: I think
from my perspective,

00:16:24.658 --> 00:16:26.970
too, I think not only
when we're open-sourcing

00:16:26.970 --> 00:16:28.440
these tools in
libraries and things

00:16:28.440 --> 00:16:30.440
like that, not only is it
for us to help other people

00:16:30.440 --> 00:16:32.565
and, like, to be part of
the open-source community,

00:16:32.565 --> 00:16:34.020
but from my
perspective, it often

00:16:34.020 --> 00:16:37.500
helps us make that tool
or that library better.

00:16:37.500 --> 00:16:39.060
I've even had, like,
several-- like,

00:16:39.060 --> 00:16:42.734
Marvin, our App Engine
framework for Go,

00:16:42.734 --> 00:16:44.400
like, immediately
after we published it,

00:16:44.400 --> 00:16:46.740
even had some nice contributors
from Google themselves

00:16:46.740 --> 00:16:49.052
that helped us clean it
up and make it better.

00:16:49.052 --> 00:16:51.510
I just love random people on
the Internet fixing my things,

00:16:51.510 --> 00:16:52.510
really.

00:16:52.510 --> 00:16:53.820
MARK MANDEL: That's super cool.

00:16:53.820 --> 00:16:55.410
You mentioned something
there, Deep, really

00:16:55.410 --> 00:16:56.330
sort of sidehandedly.

00:16:56.330 --> 00:16:59.310
Like, you said you're still
running a little bit on AWS.

00:16:59.310 --> 00:17:00.899
Are you still also
running on Prem?

00:17:00.899 --> 00:17:02.940
Like, are you running your
own data center still?

00:17:02.940 --> 00:17:04.606
Did you end up finishing
that migration?

00:17:04.606 --> 00:17:05.609
What happened there?

00:17:05.609 --> 00:17:08.190
DEEP KAPADIA: So no, we haven't
finished the migration yet.

00:17:08.190 --> 00:17:11.790
We are slated to finish it in
the second quarter of 2018.

00:17:11.790 --> 00:17:15.640
We are still in the middle of
this huge, huge migration from,

00:17:15.640 --> 00:17:16.140
like--

00:17:16.140 --> 00:17:18.390
our data centers is
completely glued to the Cloud.

00:17:18.390 --> 00:17:19.890
JP ROBINSON: Yeah,
we just finally--

00:17:19.890 --> 00:17:21.839
I think, games, we
are the first people,

00:17:21.839 --> 00:17:23.970
I think, to get complete
public traffic off

00:17:23.970 --> 00:17:26.170
of AWS in the past
couple of weeks now,

00:17:26.170 --> 00:17:27.450
which has been really nice.

00:17:27.450 --> 00:17:28.560
DEEP KAPADIA: To give
you more context, though,

00:17:28.560 --> 00:17:31.450
like, our website is going
to be hosted on GKE, as well.

00:17:31.450 --> 00:17:33.990
So if you go to nytimes.com
on your mobile phone,

00:17:33.990 --> 00:17:38.055
you are hitting a GKE
cluster that point.

00:17:38.055 --> 00:17:39.090
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:17:39.090 --> 00:17:41.880
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK, so you've
mentioned that you have systems

00:17:41.880 --> 00:17:43.500
running on Google
Cloud platform,

00:17:43.500 --> 00:17:45.720
you have systems
running on Amazon,

00:17:45.720 --> 00:17:49.830
and you also have systems
running on Premise.

00:17:49.830 --> 00:17:51.930
Now, the question
is, how do you even

00:17:51.930 --> 00:17:54.570
monitor all of these
things together?

00:17:54.570 --> 00:17:56.460
DEEP KAPADIA: So
that's a hard one,

00:17:56.460 --> 00:18:00.000
because a lot of these systems
are at very different states

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:01.700
of maturity.

00:18:01.700 --> 00:18:05.460
So like, we do have an
infrastructure and a systems

00:18:05.460 --> 00:18:08.789
team that was already
managing our data center, that

00:18:08.789 --> 00:18:10.830
was actually building
things for our data center,

00:18:10.830 --> 00:18:12.660
and managing things
in our data center.

00:18:12.660 --> 00:18:14.910
So they continue to
exist at this point,

00:18:14.910 --> 00:18:18.570
and will continue to exist
for the foreseeable future,

00:18:18.570 --> 00:18:20.760
because, you know,
there is a lot of craft

00:18:20.760 --> 00:18:23.340
already out there
in our data centers.

00:18:23.340 --> 00:18:25.770
And a lot of these systems
are going to be forklifted.

00:18:25.770 --> 00:18:27.990
So, you know, things
that were running on VMs

00:18:27.990 --> 00:18:31.860
will probably continue to run
on VMs unless we re-platform

00:18:31.860 --> 00:18:35.610
them to either run on something
like App Engine or Containers.

00:18:35.610 --> 00:18:38.690
So that's not going away.

00:18:38.690 --> 00:18:42.480
We have the traditional way
of maintaining and managing

00:18:42.480 --> 00:18:44.140
the systems in our data centers.

00:18:44.140 --> 00:18:46.650
For AWS, there is a little
bit more automation.

00:18:46.650 --> 00:18:49.950
We were using, or
we still are using,

00:18:49.950 --> 00:18:53.790
Puppet and some
configuration management

00:18:53.790 --> 00:18:57.620
tools to manage some of
the systems in Amazon.

00:18:57.620 --> 00:19:00.600
However, on the Google Cloud,
we've sort of gone all in

00:19:00.600 --> 00:19:03.000
and said, like, you know,
we're going to do Kubernetes,

00:19:03.000 --> 00:19:04.375
and we're going
to do Containers,

00:19:04.375 --> 00:19:06.170
and, like, you know,
use Drone, and, like,

00:19:06.170 --> 00:19:10.260
have this faster CI
pipeline that allows

00:19:10.260 --> 00:19:12.090
us to deploy stuff to Google.

00:19:12.090 --> 00:19:15.630
Over time, though, we hope
that, like, once we're fully

00:19:15.630 --> 00:19:18.380
in the Cloud, both
Google and Amazon,

00:19:18.380 --> 00:19:21.330
we can use certain
abstractions that

00:19:21.330 --> 00:19:25.230
allow us to sort of deploy code,
either in Google or Amazon,

00:19:25.230 --> 00:19:26.470
the same way.

00:19:26.470 --> 00:19:30.030
So one of the ways
that we are trying

00:19:30.030 --> 00:19:33.420
to rationalize some of the
infrastructure automation

00:19:33.420 --> 00:19:36.540
is by using tools
like Terraform,

00:19:36.540 --> 00:19:39.246
which has support for both
Google as well as Amazon.

00:19:39.246 --> 00:19:41.370
You know, again, one of
the choices with Kubernetes

00:19:41.370 --> 00:19:44.400
is, as I said, was, you
know, it could run either

00:19:44.400 --> 00:19:45.930
in Google or Amazon.

00:19:45.930 --> 00:19:48.420
So as we sort of get more and
more systems in the Cloud,

00:19:48.420 --> 00:19:51.134
we want to converge
ways we manage things.

00:19:51.134 --> 00:19:52.800
JP ROBINSON: I think
one interesting way

00:19:52.800 --> 00:19:55.620
that we've also tried to manage,
like, part of this transition

00:19:55.620 --> 00:19:59.610
is that as services in Google
are needing services in Amazon,

00:19:59.610 --> 00:20:02.520
we're actually running
Cloud Endpoints in AWS

00:20:02.520 --> 00:20:04.466
to expose those services.

00:20:04.466 --> 00:20:06.090
So it's actually been
a nice way for us

00:20:06.090 --> 00:20:09.120
to get out of the mentality
that everybody's really

00:20:09.120 --> 00:20:12.660
defense via firewalls to
actually expose ourselves

00:20:12.660 --> 00:20:15.189
to the public securely
using common tools.

00:20:15.189 --> 00:20:15.980
DEEP KAPADIA: Yeah.

00:20:15.980 --> 00:20:17.354
There's actually
a story to that.

00:20:17.354 --> 00:20:19.680
And I don't know-- like, I
know you guys like stories.

00:20:19.680 --> 00:20:20.445
So--

00:20:20.445 --> 00:20:21.820
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
We love stories.

00:20:21.820 --> 00:20:22.506
MARK MANDEL: We love stories.

00:20:22.506 --> 00:20:23.400
Go ahead with the story.

00:20:23.400 --> 00:20:24.858
DEEP KAPADIA:
--like, when we first

00:20:24.858 --> 00:20:27.720
started looking at Google,
as I said, like, you know,

00:20:27.720 --> 00:20:30.570
we've traditionally done
things a certain way.

00:20:30.570 --> 00:20:33.350
And we were trying to sort
of replicate our data center

00:20:33.350 --> 00:20:33.960
in Amazon.

00:20:33.960 --> 00:20:37.620
So like, any app deployed
to Amazon or our data center

00:20:37.620 --> 00:20:40.072
should be indistinguishable
from each other, right?

00:20:40.072 --> 00:20:42.280
That was sort of the philosophy
we wanted to go with.

00:20:42.280 --> 00:20:43.738
So we tried to do
that with Google.

00:20:43.738 --> 00:20:45.560
We quickly learned
that, like, you know,

00:20:45.560 --> 00:20:49.110
we were basically fighting
what was inevitable.

00:20:49.110 --> 00:20:52.980
Like, with Google, like, it
assumed, like, a zero trust

00:20:52.980 --> 00:20:53.480
network.

00:20:53.480 --> 00:20:55.980
There was no clear notion of
private networking at the time.

00:20:55.980 --> 00:20:58.530
Like, now, I know you guys have
EPCs and Peering, and things

00:20:58.530 --> 00:20:59.380
like that.

00:20:59.380 --> 00:21:01.740
But like we were talking
about a year ago, like,

00:21:01.740 --> 00:21:03.210
GCP didn't have that.

00:21:03.210 --> 00:21:05.250
So there was no easy
way for applications

00:21:05.250 --> 00:21:06.690
to talk to each
other that existed

00:21:06.690 --> 00:21:09.450
in different projects,
or applications

00:21:09.450 --> 00:21:11.700
that we were building in
GCP to talk to services that

00:21:11.700 --> 00:21:13.590
were sitting in our data center,
because our data center was

00:21:13.590 --> 00:21:16.290
behind, like, a firewall,
and we couldn't, like, really

00:21:16.290 --> 00:21:18.480
open those ports up.

00:21:18.480 --> 00:21:20.287
So what we started
doing was, you

00:21:20.287 --> 00:21:21.960
know, we found a
novel way around it,

00:21:21.960 --> 00:21:23.820
which was using Google
Cloud endpoints.

00:21:23.820 --> 00:21:26.250
And it allowed us to
basically stand up something

00:21:26.250 --> 00:21:30.780
in our Amazon environment using
the Cloud endpoints service

00:21:30.780 --> 00:21:35.070
proxy to actually proxy all
the traffic to our data centers

00:21:35.070 --> 00:21:35.609
from Google.

00:21:35.609 --> 00:21:38.025
So the teams that were moving
at a faster pace in Google--

00:21:38.025 --> 00:21:38.520
MARK MANDEL: That's cool.

00:21:38.520 --> 00:21:41.040
DEEP KAPADIA: --were able to
continue building in Google

00:21:41.040 --> 00:21:44.700
by exposing some of the
underlying services in our data

00:21:44.700 --> 00:21:46.350
center and Amazon.

00:21:46.350 --> 00:21:48.027
So that was our learning.

00:21:48.027 --> 00:21:50.610
Like, you know, like, if teams
want to move at their own pace,

00:21:50.610 --> 00:21:52.443
they can continue to
move at their own pace.

00:21:52.443 --> 00:21:55.530
And let's not try and fight this
whole, like, private networking

00:21:55.530 --> 00:21:57.640
battle in Google
because you know what,

00:21:57.640 --> 00:21:59.292
let's just assume
zero trust network.

00:21:59.292 --> 00:22:00.250
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Great.

00:22:00.250 --> 00:22:02.730
So what you're saying is
that you are basically

00:22:02.730 --> 00:22:05.970
exposing most of your
APIs in the open,

00:22:05.970 --> 00:22:07.050
but you're doing so in--

00:22:07.050 --> 00:22:07.370
DEEP KAPADIA: Yes.

00:22:07.370 --> 00:22:08.740
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
--in a secure way.

00:22:08.740 --> 00:22:10.650
So I'm curious about,
how are you doing that?

00:22:10.650 --> 00:22:12.390
Are you doing OAuth 2?

00:22:12.390 --> 00:22:15.676
What exact authentication
system are you using there?

00:22:15.676 --> 00:22:16.800
DEEP KAPADIA: It's mostly--

00:22:16.800 --> 00:22:19.320
like, some of the
APIs were retrofitted

00:22:19.320 --> 00:22:23.340
to, like, have, like, auth
token in front of them.

00:22:23.340 --> 00:22:26.650
Others that could sort of be
modified a little bit more

00:22:26.650 --> 00:22:29.850
are using JWP to
secure themselves.

00:22:29.850 --> 00:22:31.720
And then, you know,
in other cases,

00:22:31.720 --> 00:22:35.320
some APIs already had
auth mechanisms built in,

00:22:35.320 --> 00:22:37.949
which allowed us to just
use the proxy as is.

00:22:37.949 --> 00:22:40.490
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And in order
to communicate with each other,

00:22:40.490 --> 00:22:44.970
you are using directly
HTTP with JASON, or--

00:22:44.970 --> 00:22:47.910
so REST APIs, are you
using any kind of GRPC,

00:22:47.910 --> 00:22:49.769
or any other RPC framework?

00:22:49.769 --> 00:22:50.810
DEEP KAPADIA: Yes and no.

00:22:50.810 --> 00:22:52.290
I mean, yes and yes.

00:22:52.290 --> 00:22:53.100
Sorry.

00:22:53.100 --> 00:22:57.030
So for the older
APIs, like, they

00:22:57.030 --> 00:22:58.710
were already JASONed over REST.

00:22:58.710 --> 00:23:01.140
We are definitely
looking into GRPC.

00:23:01.140 --> 00:23:05.880
I know some of JP's APIs
are already built with GRPC

00:23:05.880 --> 00:23:07.539
as the transport mechanism.

00:23:07.539 --> 00:23:09.580
And JP can tell you a
little bit more about that.

00:23:09.580 --> 00:23:12.750
But we also recently released
our publishing pipeline,

00:23:12.750 --> 00:23:17.700
which is basically the content
pipeline from when a reporter

00:23:17.700 --> 00:23:21.390
and editor, like, enter
a story into our CMS,

00:23:21.390 --> 00:23:25.290
it has to basically go through
a whole workflow, where

00:23:25.290 --> 00:23:28.124
it gets published to a
pipeline and, like, there

00:23:28.124 --> 00:23:29.790
are all these consumers
that are waiting

00:23:29.790 --> 00:23:33.030
for, like, the new edits
or new updates to a story,

00:23:33.030 --> 00:23:37.440
or an entirely new story, or
any new assets to a story.

00:23:37.440 --> 00:23:39.870
They're all listening
to this pipeline.

00:23:39.870 --> 00:23:43.580
And transport mechanism
for the pipeline is GRPC.

00:23:43.580 --> 00:23:46.740
And the pipeline, basically,
is a large message log

00:23:46.740 --> 00:23:48.430
sitting on top of KAFKA.

00:23:48.430 --> 00:23:48.750
MARK MANDEL: Interesting.

00:23:48.750 --> 00:23:50.940
JP, can you tell us a bit
more about your experience

00:23:50.940 --> 00:23:51.360
with the GRPC?

00:23:51.360 --> 00:23:52.350
I'd be curious to know.

00:23:52.350 --> 00:23:53.349
JP ROBINSON: Sure, sure.

00:23:53.349 --> 00:23:56.310
So like I said,
most of my services

00:23:56.310 --> 00:24:00.030
are running on App Engine, where
most of our external traffic

00:24:00.030 --> 00:24:02.257
is actually coming
in from user facing

00:24:02.257 --> 00:24:03.340
apps and things like that.

00:24:03.340 --> 00:24:05.310
So most of that mechanism--
most of our authentication

00:24:05.310 --> 00:24:06.990
and things like that are just
using our existing New York

00:24:06.990 --> 00:24:08.411
Times user authentication.

00:24:08.411 --> 00:24:10.660
It gets interesting when we
start going to the service

00:24:10.660 --> 00:24:13.080
to service type
of authentication.

00:24:13.080 --> 00:24:14.940
When it comes to
App Engine itself,

00:24:14.940 --> 00:24:17.840
we actually get to rely on
that one specific header.

00:24:17.840 --> 00:24:19.610
It's like the X App ID header.

00:24:19.610 --> 00:24:21.120
And we can just
guarantee that that

00:24:21.120 --> 00:24:24.990
will sign the incoming request,
and we know it's from our app.

00:24:24.990 --> 00:24:28.080
Kubernetes, it starts getting
fun with GRPC, though.

00:24:28.080 --> 00:24:30.600
We recently launched
one service where

00:24:30.600 --> 00:24:33.930
it is a GRPC service underneath
the hood with Cloud endpoints

00:24:33.930 --> 00:24:36.060
on the front, running on GKE.

00:24:36.060 --> 00:24:39.420
Since we are also calling
this service from App Engine,

00:24:39.420 --> 00:24:41.610
we're actually also
leaning on the fun feature

00:24:41.610 --> 00:24:44.970
that Cloud endpoints
does, the HTTP JSON

00:24:44.970 --> 00:24:46.591
translation to GRPC for us.

00:24:46.591 --> 00:24:47.340
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:24:47.340 --> 00:24:49.631
JP ROBINSON: And that actually
kind of ties back to one

00:24:49.631 --> 00:24:51.990
of our open-source tools
called Open API to Proto,

00:24:51.990 --> 00:24:55.380
where we actually describe
our API in an OpenAPI spec,

00:24:55.380 --> 00:24:58.140
and generate Protobufs that
mimic that OpenAPI spec--

00:24:58.140 --> 00:24:59.056
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:24:59.056 --> 00:25:01.590
JP ROBINSON: --with
the HTTP annotations.

00:25:01.590 --> 00:25:04.636
So we can describe our service
in an OpenAPI spec translated

00:25:04.636 --> 00:25:06.260
to Protobuf, handed
to Cloud endpoints,

00:25:06.260 --> 00:25:09.790
in that it's exposed in both
of those transport styles quite

00:25:09.790 --> 00:25:10.540
seamlessly for us.

00:25:10.540 --> 00:25:11.425
It's kind of nice.

00:25:11.425 --> 00:25:12.550
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That's really cool.

00:25:12.550 --> 00:25:13.690
MARK MANDEL: That's very cool.

00:25:13.690 --> 00:25:16.189
As much as I'd love to talk to
you both about all this stuff

00:25:16.189 --> 00:25:18.535
forever, we are running out
of time just a little bit--

00:25:18.535 --> 00:25:20.180
oh, actually, quite a lot.

00:25:20.180 --> 00:25:22.900
So before we wrap up,
is there anything else

00:25:22.900 --> 00:25:25.690
that you might want to
tell people, or to plug,

00:25:25.690 --> 00:25:27.820
or if there's something
particularly amazing

00:25:27.820 --> 00:25:29.080
that you've done, or
a lesson that you've

00:25:29.080 --> 00:25:31.450
learned that you want to
share that we haven't managed

00:25:31.450 --> 00:25:32.650
to touch on yet?

00:25:32.650 --> 00:25:34.150
Is there anything
you'd like to add?

00:25:34.150 --> 00:25:36.100
JP, we have you right now.

00:25:36.100 --> 00:25:36.880
JP ROBINSON: Sure.

00:25:36.880 --> 00:25:41.680
For me, honestly, moving all the
games platform to Google Cloud

00:25:41.680 --> 00:25:44.610
has been a rather wonderful
experience, honestly.

00:25:44.610 --> 00:25:47.980
We just get to worry about
our product and the problems

00:25:47.980 --> 00:25:49.930
we should be solving,
and not necessarily

00:25:49.930 --> 00:25:52.090
common infrastructure
problems and the like.

00:25:52.090 --> 00:25:53.560
It's actually been quite nice.

00:25:53.560 --> 00:25:56.102
And with a team of two people,
we are able to do quite a lot.

00:25:56.102 --> 00:25:57.059
MARK MANDEL: Excellent.

00:25:57.059 --> 00:25:58.030
Deep, yourself.

00:25:58.030 --> 00:25:59.890
DEEP KAPADIA: What I've
learned is, I think,

00:25:59.890 --> 00:26:02.950
we want to build more
tooling around being

00:26:02.950 --> 00:26:05.084
able to help teams move faster.

00:26:05.084 --> 00:26:07.000
So I think that's sort
of where we want to be.

00:26:07.000 --> 00:26:08.890
Like, we want to
shift our mentality

00:26:08.890 --> 00:26:13.780
from being traditional,
like, systems people--

00:26:13.780 --> 00:26:16.870
like, you know, people
who manage infrastructure,

00:26:16.870 --> 00:26:20.680
to people who actually build
automation around the Google

00:26:20.680 --> 00:26:21.756
infrastructure.

00:26:21.756 --> 00:26:24.130
So I think that, to us, has
been a very interesting shift

00:26:24.130 --> 00:26:25.550
in mentality.

00:26:25.550 --> 00:26:27.580
And I think if there are
other organizations who

00:26:27.580 --> 00:26:30.774
are trying to make
the shift, I think

00:26:30.774 --> 00:26:33.190
that's where, like, you know,
they will find a lot of bang

00:26:33.190 --> 00:26:34.840
for their buck,
and like, you know,

00:26:34.840 --> 00:26:38.080
in terms of investing in talent.

00:26:38.080 --> 00:26:39.580
I think we've made
that investment,

00:26:39.580 --> 00:26:41.470
and I think we are reaping
the benefits, at this point.

00:26:41.470 --> 00:26:43.460
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, I
wanted to add one more thing,

00:26:43.460 --> 00:26:45.880
which is, for everyone that
is interested in the New York

00:26:45.880 --> 00:26:47.560
Times and everything
you're doing tech,

00:26:47.560 --> 00:26:50.067
you do have open.nytimes.com--

00:26:50.067 --> 00:26:50.900
DEEP KAPADIA: We do.

00:26:50.900 --> 00:26:53.275
FRANCESC CAMPOY: --which is
basically where we're taking,

00:26:53.275 --> 00:26:55.600
like, half of our cool
things of the week.

00:26:55.600 --> 00:26:57.290
MARK MANDEL: Yes.

00:26:57.290 --> 00:26:59.620
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So yeah,
I just wanted to say--

00:26:59.620 --> 00:27:02.044
so JP Robinson, you
read a lot in there.

00:27:02.044 --> 00:27:03.460
But there's also
other people that

00:27:03.460 --> 00:27:04.918
write not only
about Go, but, like,

00:27:04.918 --> 00:27:06.760
also CSS and stuff like that.

00:27:06.760 --> 00:27:09.040
So I just wanted to
thank all of the authors

00:27:09.040 --> 00:27:11.272
in there, because it is
really, really cool content.

00:27:11.272 --> 00:27:13.230
JP ROBINSON: Awesome,
thanks for the shout out.

00:27:13.230 --> 00:27:13.980
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:27:13.980 --> 00:27:16.962
Well, yeah, thanks to both of
you, Deep, JP, for joining us.

00:27:16.962 --> 00:27:19.420
I really appreciate you taking
the time to hang out with us

00:27:19.420 --> 00:27:22.180
and chat about what everything
you're doing at New York Times.

00:27:22.180 --> 00:27:22.960
DEEP KAPADIA: Thank you so much.

00:27:22.960 --> 00:27:23.740
JP ROBINSON: Yeah, our pleasure.

00:27:23.740 --> 00:27:24.239
Thanks.

00:27:24.239 --> 00:27:25.510
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Thank you.

00:27:25.510 --> 00:27:27.280
MELANIE WARRICK:
Thanks, JP and Deep.

00:27:27.280 --> 00:27:30.400
We appreciate the insights on
how you guys are using GCP.

00:27:30.400 --> 00:27:32.680
So, Mark, let's get to
the question of the week.

00:27:32.680 --> 00:27:35.470
How do you secure
Kubernetes' cluster?

00:27:35.470 --> 00:27:39.100
What are some best practices to
securing a Kubernetes cluster?

00:27:39.100 --> 00:27:40.990
MARK MANDEL: I'm glad
you asked, because I'm

00:27:40.990 --> 00:27:43.900
able to reference a wonderful
blog post that has been written

00:27:43.900 --> 00:27:44.720
on the GCP blog

00:27:44.720 --> 00:27:45.550
MELANIE WARRICK:
That's very helpful.

00:27:45.550 --> 00:27:47.590
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, from
product manager Aaron Small.

00:27:47.590 --> 00:27:49.540
Yeah, it's great when other
people do my research for me.

00:27:49.540 --> 00:27:50.425
MELANIE WARRICK: Exactly.

00:27:50.425 --> 00:27:52.300
MARK MANDEL: No, it's
actually-- there's a bunch

00:27:52.300 --> 00:27:54.220
of different things that you
can do in terms of security

00:27:54.220 --> 00:27:56.380
best practices for your
Kubernetes cluster.

00:27:56.380 --> 00:27:59.170
I will definitely put a link in
the show notes to the blog post

00:27:59.170 --> 00:28:00.400
so you can read it yourself.

00:28:00.400 --> 00:28:01.420
There's a few things
in here that I

00:28:01.420 --> 00:28:02.650
think are particularly good.

00:28:02.650 --> 00:28:04.024
There's a bunch
of stuff in there

00:28:04.024 --> 00:28:06.610
about using least privileged
service accounts on your nodes.

00:28:06.610 --> 00:28:08.350
So basically, when
you set up the nodes

00:28:08.350 --> 00:28:09.724
with your new
Kubernetes cluster,

00:28:09.724 --> 00:28:12.370
you can give them permission
to do certain things

00:28:12.370 --> 00:28:13.432
within your cluster.

00:28:13.432 --> 00:28:14.890
You may or may not
want to do that.

00:28:14.890 --> 00:28:18.040
So if there's no
necessary reason for, say,

00:28:18.040 --> 00:28:20.770
a particular node to access
Pub/Sub, for example, or Google

00:28:20.770 --> 00:28:23.320
Cloud Storage, maybe there's
no reason for them to do that,

00:28:23.320 --> 00:28:25.030
then you might want
to turn that off.

00:28:25.030 --> 00:28:27.630
If there's no reason for them
to be accessing other Compute

00:28:27.630 --> 00:28:29.380
Engine instances, you
can set up firewalls

00:28:29.380 --> 00:28:30.640
to do that sort of thing.

00:28:30.640 --> 00:28:33.489
Other nice stuff in there,
there is a Kubernetes web UI.

00:28:33.489 --> 00:28:34.780
You can actually turn that off.

00:28:34.780 --> 00:28:36.820
There's a command line
here to turn off the UI.

00:28:36.820 --> 00:28:39.350
The UI has administrative access
to do pretty much anything.

00:28:39.350 --> 00:28:41.710
So if somebody gets access to
it, which they shouldn't, you

00:28:41.710 --> 00:28:42.370
can turn that off.

00:28:42.370 --> 00:28:44.744
That means that then, you can
look down how things happen

00:28:44.744 --> 00:28:46.460
inside your Kubernetes cluster.

00:28:46.460 --> 00:28:50.080
And it's also worth noting
that the attribute based

00:28:50.080 --> 00:28:53.460
access control was disabled
by default in Kubernetes 1.8.

00:28:53.460 --> 00:28:56.020
And that gave a lot more
access to Kubernetes.

00:28:56.020 --> 00:28:58.780
Now, R-back, which is the
role based access control,

00:28:58.780 --> 00:29:00.490
was released as beta
in Kubernetes 1.6,

00:29:00.490 --> 00:29:01.655
is now the default in 1.8.

00:29:01.655 --> 00:29:04.030
And there's commands in here
if you want to turn that off

00:29:04.030 --> 00:29:06.850
for existing-- if you want to
turn off that attribute based

00:29:06.850 --> 00:29:08.130
access control.

00:29:08.130 --> 00:29:09.385
Anyway, good for R-back.

00:29:09.385 --> 00:29:10.510
R-back is the one you want.

00:29:10.510 --> 00:29:11.260
That is the one you want.

00:29:11.260 --> 00:29:13.060
MELANIE WARRICK: So basically,
you can turn everything off--

00:29:13.060 --> 00:29:13.530
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:29:13.530 --> 00:29:14.946
MELANIE WARRICK:
--and cap it all,

00:29:14.946 --> 00:29:16.810
to keep it from anybody
accessing things

00:29:16.810 --> 00:29:17.860
you're not even using.

00:29:17.860 --> 00:29:19.120
MARK MANDEL: Exactly, yeah.

